1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,081
In 1943, a family from New York City made

2
00:00:04,101 --> 00:00:06,821
the long trip south to a prison camp in

3
00:00:06,841 --> 00:00:08,343
the cotton country of Texas.

4
00:00:08,363 --> 00:00:10,474
They had come to see their son.

5
00:00:10,494 --> 00:00:12,930
He was a corporal in the German army.

6
00:00:14,250 --> 00:00:15,690
They brought him presents.

7
00:00:15,710 --> 00:00:18,610
A sewing machine, because he was a tailor.

8
00:00:18,630 --> 00:00:19,705
And a radio.

9
00:00:19,725 --> 00:00:22,260
In the barracks around him lived thousands

10
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:25,910
of men who had fought for Hitler in North Africa.

11
00:00:25,930 --> 00:00:29,560
Most of them did not like what he did with

12
00:00:29,580 --> 00:00:30,380
that radio.

13
00:00:31,575 --> 00:00:33,502
On the night of December seventeenth,

14
00:00:33,522 --> 00:00:36,491
1943, a little before midnight,

15
00:00:36,511 --> 00:00:38,970
men with clubs walked into his barracks.

16
00:00:38,990 --> 00:00:41,448
The attack lasted less than a minute.

17
00:00:41,468 --> 00:00:44,655
Nobody in the room got up to help him.

18
00:00:45,875 --> 00:00:47,761
This is the documented story of Hugo

19
00:00:47,781 --> 00:00:49,939
Krauss, the boy from New York who came

20
00:00:49,959 --> 00:00:51,845
back to America as a German prisoner,

21
00:00:51,865 --> 00:00:54,023
of the men who killed him for it,

22
00:00:54,043 --> 00:00:56,746
and of a stranger on a Texas road who made

23
00:00:56,766 --> 00:00:57,855
one of them confess.

24
00:00:59,650 --> 00:01:01,773
Hugo Krauss was born in Germany,

25
00:01:01,793 --> 00:01:02,870
in 1919.

26
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:06,122
When he was nine, his family crossed the

27
00:01:06,142 --> 00:01:08,154
Atlantic and settled in Manhattan,

28
00:01:08,174 --> 00:01:10,593
in Yorkville, the German neighborhood of

29
00:01:10,613 --> 00:01:11,405
New York.

30
00:01:11,425 --> 00:01:13,438
He went to school there.

31
00:01:13,458 --> 00:01:15,490
He grew up speaking English.

32
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:19,875
In 1939, when he was nineteen,

33
00:01:19,895 --> 00:01:21,669
he sailed back to Germany.

34
00:01:21,689 --> 00:01:23,463
A pro-Nazi group in America,

35
00:01:23,483 --> 00:01:25,975
the German American Bund, helped pay for

36
00:01:25,995 --> 00:01:26,692
the trip.

37
00:01:26,712 --> 00:01:29,563
One history says he went as a Nazi

38
00:01:29,583 --> 00:01:32,074
supporter, another says he went to visit

39
00:01:32,094 --> 00:01:32,433
relatives.

40
00:01:32,453 --> 00:01:35,304
Why he went, and what he believed then,

41
00:01:35,324 --> 00:01:36,400
is not clear.

42
00:01:37,725 --> 00:01:39,352
Then the war began.

43
00:01:39,372 --> 00:01:41,410
The German army drafted him.

44
00:01:41,430 --> 00:01:45,938
At first he got out of it by saying he was

45
00:01:45,958 --> 00:01:47,173
an American citizen.

46
00:01:47,193 --> 00:01:48,820
Later he joined anyway.

47
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:52,545
He fought in Russia, and then in North Africa.

48
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:57,606
In the spring of 1943, the German army in

49
00:01:57,626 --> 00:01:58,299
Tunisia surrendered.

50
00:01:58,319 --> 00:02:00,031
Krauss was captured with it.

51
00:02:00,051 --> 00:02:03,149
Less than four years after he had left New

52
00:02:03,169 --> 00:02:05,575
York, he was crossing the Atlantic again,

53
00:02:05,595 --> 00:02:08,020
this time as a prisoner of war.

54
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:11,640
He was sent to a town called Hearne,

55
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:13,648
in central Texas, sixty-five miles

56
00:02:13,668 --> 00:02:16,458
southeast of Waco, on the bottomland of

57
00:02:16,478 --> 00:02:17,627
the Brazos River.

58
00:02:17,647 --> 00:02:19,949
About three thousand five hundred people

59
00:02:19,969 --> 00:02:23,046
lived there, with two cotton gins and two

60
00:02:23,066 --> 00:02:23,866
railroad lines.

61
00:02:25,150 --> 00:02:27,900
On the edge of town stood one of the

62
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:30,055
largest prisoner of war camps in the

63
00:02:30,075 --> 00:02:30,671
United States.

64
00:02:30,691 --> 00:02:32,870
The first men from the Afrika Korps

65
00:02:32,890 --> 00:02:36,290
arrived in June 1943.

66
00:02:36,310 --> 00:02:38,797
At its peak, Camp Hearne held almost five

67
00:02:38,817 --> 00:02:40,543
thousand prisoners, in three compounds

68
00:02:40,563 --> 00:02:41,610
behind the wire.

69
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:44,967
Life there was not hard.

70
00:02:44,987 --> 00:02:46,596
The prisoners built theaters,

71
00:02:46,616 --> 00:02:48,225
and played in orchestras.

72
00:02:48,245 --> 00:02:50,669
They built small castles, waist high,

73
00:02:50,689 --> 00:02:52,298
with towers and moats.

74
00:02:52,318 --> 00:02:54,742
They picked cotton for local farmers,

75
00:02:54,762 --> 00:02:55,557
for wages.

76
00:02:55,577 --> 00:02:58,815
Some people in town called the camp the

77
00:02:58,835 --> 00:02:59,650
Fritz Ritz.

78
00:03:00,950 --> 00:03:03,425
But inside the compounds, the hard Nazis

79
00:03:03,445 --> 00:03:05,564
kept order among their own men.

80
00:03:05,584 --> 00:03:07,703
Prisoners who doubted Hitler kept quiet.

81
00:03:07,723 --> 00:03:10,555
Those who did not could be beaten at

82
00:03:10,575 --> 00:03:13,070
night, by men from their own company.

83
00:03:14,750 --> 00:03:16,854
Hugo Krauss did not keep quiet.

84
00:03:16,874 --> 00:03:18,979
He spoke perfect English, and the

85
00:03:18,999 --> 00:03:20,749
Americans made him an interpreter.

86
00:03:20,769 --> 00:03:23,227
He told the other prisoners that Germany

87
00:03:23,247 --> 00:03:25,352
was going to lose the war,

88
00:03:25,372 --> 00:03:27,850
and that he was not going back.

89
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:31,714
He said he would get leave at Christmas,

90
00:03:31,734 --> 00:03:34,018
to visit his parents in New York.

91
00:03:34,038 --> 00:03:36,982
He said he wanted to join the American army.

92
00:03:37,002 --> 00:03:39,286
When his company sang German songs after

93
00:03:39,306 --> 00:03:41,940
meals, he got up and left the room.

94
00:03:43,225 --> 00:03:45,673
He had a sewing machine, and he made

95
00:03:45,693 --> 00:03:47,524
shirts and hats for other prisoners.

96
00:03:47,544 --> 00:03:49,066
And he had his radio.

97
00:03:49,086 --> 00:03:50,609
He listened to American news,

98
00:03:50,629 --> 00:03:53,385
and told the men around him that it was

99
00:03:53,406 --> 00:03:55,565
more accurate than the news from Germany.

100
00:03:56,950 --> 00:03:59,310
A man in his barracks, another interpreter

101
00:03:59,330 --> 00:04:01,947
named Heino Erichsen, later remembered the

102
00:04:01,967 --> 00:04:02,239
visitors.

103
00:04:02,259 --> 00:04:04,285
Krauss had American relatives who came to

104
00:04:04,305 --> 00:04:06,915
see him in the camp, and some of the

105
00:04:06,935 --> 00:04:10,150
prisoners said a man like that had to be a spy.

106
00:04:11,475 --> 00:04:13,654
The army later put it simply.

107
00:04:13,674 --> 00:04:15,486
Krauss was very much disliked,

108
00:04:15,506 --> 00:04:18,051
a report said, and considered a traitor,

109
00:04:18,071 --> 00:04:19,883
especially by the younger prisoners,

110
00:04:19,903 --> 00:04:21,735
who were devoted to Hitler.

111
00:04:23,450 --> 00:04:27,146
By December 1943, the Nazis in his

112
00:04:27,166 --> 00:04:29,301
compound held two things against him.

113
00:04:29,321 --> 00:04:31,455
Some German sergeants had been organizing

114
00:04:31,475 --> 00:04:33,251
slowdowns in the work program,

115
00:04:33,271 --> 00:04:36,124
and about a week before the attack they

116
00:04:36,144 --> 00:04:37,919
were moved to another camp.

117
00:04:37,939 --> 00:04:40,433
The Nazis believed Krauss had told the

118
00:04:40,453 --> 00:04:41,530
Americans about them.

119
00:04:42,725 --> 00:04:45,801
Then the guards found a secret shortwave

120
00:04:45,821 --> 00:04:48,897
radio, hidden by prisoners to listen to

121
00:04:48,917 --> 00:04:50,224
news from Germany.

122
00:04:50,244 --> 00:04:52,436
The Americans took it away.

123
00:04:52,456 --> 00:04:54,225
The prisoners blamed Krauss.

124
00:04:54,925 --> 00:04:56,885
On December seventeenth, the ranking

125
00:04:56,905 --> 00:04:59,405
sergeant of his company, Anton Boehmer,

126
00:04:59,705 --> 00:05:02,060
stood in front of the men and announced

127
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:04,733
that there was a man among them who was

128
00:05:04,753 --> 00:05:05,920
disloyal to the cause.

129
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:07,425
Everyone understood who he meant.

130
00:05:08,650 --> 00:05:11,237
Krauss heard what was being said.

131
00:05:11,257 --> 00:05:12,975
According to one prisoner,

132
00:05:12,995 --> 00:05:14,279
he answered loudly.

133
00:05:14,299 --> 00:05:16,452
Let them come, he said.

134
00:05:16,472 --> 00:05:18,210
I am not afraid.

135
00:05:19,975 --> 00:05:22,402
That evening, a group met in another

136
00:05:22,422 --> 00:05:23,451
barracks, by candlelight.

137
00:05:23,471 --> 00:05:24,500
They had sticks.

138
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:27,296
Some of the sticks had long spikes driven

139
00:05:27,316 --> 00:05:27,995
through them.

140
00:05:28,015 --> 00:05:30,442
One man had a piece of pipe.

141
00:05:30,462 --> 00:05:32,890
Lookouts went out to watch the guard

142
00:05:32,910 --> 00:05:36,036
towers, and a man was chosen to turn off

143
00:05:36,056 --> 00:05:36,856
the lights.

144
00:05:38,075 --> 00:05:40,173
At about a quarter to midnight,

145
00:05:40,193 --> 00:05:41,938
they walked into Barracks One.

146
00:05:41,958 --> 00:05:43,351
The lights went out.

147
00:05:43,371 --> 00:05:46,195
Krauss was in his bunk near the door.

148
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:50,270
Heino Erichsen was in the same barracks

149
00:05:50,290 --> 00:05:50,970
that night.

150
00:05:50,990 --> 00:05:53,420
He remembered the noise, the lights going

151
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:56,570
out, the screaming, and how quickly it was over.

152
00:05:56,590 --> 00:05:59,020
The attack lasted less than a minute.

153
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:01,470
Krauss cried out for it to stop.

154
00:06:01,490 --> 00:06:04,640
No one in the room came to help him.

155
00:06:05,875 --> 00:06:08,403
His skull was broken in three places.

156
00:06:08,423 --> 00:06:10,587
Both of his arms were broken,

157
00:06:10,607 --> 00:06:12,407
and bones in his hand.

158
00:06:12,427 --> 00:06:14,954
He was taken to the camp hospital,

159
00:06:14,974 --> 00:06:18,230
and the next day to an army hospital in

160
00:06:18,250 --> 00:06:18,958
Temple, Texas.

161
00:06:18,978 --> 00:06:21,142
He died there on December twenty-third,

162
00:06:21,162 --> 00:06:24,895
1943, a month short of his twenty-fifth birthday.

163
00:06:26,150 --> 00:06:28,834
Years later, some of the American guards

164
00:06:28,854 --> 00:06:31,538
told much worse stories about that night.

165
00:06:31,558 --> 00:06:33,856
The historians who studied the medical

166
00:06:33,876 --> 00:06:36,174
report and the autopsy photographs found

167
00:06:36,194 --> 00:06:38,492
that those things did not happen.

168
00:06:38,512 --> 00:06:40,830
Rumors had grown in the retelling.

169
00:06:42,575 --> 00:06:44,139
The Army investigated.

170
00:06:44,159 --> 00:06:46,779
Almost fifty prisoners were questioned.

171
00:06:46,799 --> 00:06:48,363
Nobody named anyone.

172
00:06:48,383 --> 00:06:53,115
In the dark, they said, they had seen nothing.

173
00:06:53,135 --> 00:06:56,390
Heino Erichsen asked to be transferred out

174
00:06:56,410 --> 00:06:58,035
of the camp the next day.

175
00:06:59,275 --> 00:07:00,905
In New York, Krauss's father,

176
00:07:00,925 --> 00:07:02,555
Henry, wrote to the Army.

177
00:07:02,575 --> 00:07:05,195
He was a citizen of the United States,

178
00:07:05,215 --> 00:07:07,505
he wrote, and he wanted a full

179
00:07:07,525 --> 00:07:09,835
investigation, and every detail sent to him.

180
00:07:11,050 --> 00:07:14,670
The answer came in 1944.

181
00:07:14,690 --> 00:07:16,432
His son's attitude and conduct,

182
00:07:16,452 --> 00:07:18,898
the Army wrote, had been praiseworthy from

183
00:07:18,918 --> 00:07:20,660
an American point of view,

184
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:23,126
and that had made the other German

185
00:07:23,146 --> 00:07:24,184
prisoners his enemies.

186
00:07:24,204 --> 00:07:26,670
The men who attacked him remained unidentified.

187
00:07:28,125 --> 00:07:31,651
In January 1945, the New York Times

188
00:07:31,671 --> 00:07:34,006
printed a short story about a former

189
00:07:34,026 --> 00:07:36,698
Yorkville man who had been killed as a

190
00:07:36,718 --> 00:07:37,707
prisoner of war.

191
00:07:37,727 --> 00:07:40,063
The war in Europe ended that spring.

192
00:07:40,083 --> 00:07:41,765
The case was still open.

193
00:07:43,450 --> 00:07:45,846
One of the attackers that night was a

194
00:07:45,866 --> 00:07:48,270
corporal named Guenther Meisel.

195
00:07:48,290 --> 00:07:50,666
He had been in the Hitler Youth.

196
00:07:50,686 --> 00:07:53,011
At Camp Hearne, he worked in the truck

197
00:07:53,031 --> 00:07:54,888
workshop, and the Army trusted him enough

198
00:07:54,908 --> 00:07:57,590
to let him drive a truck alone between the camps.

199
00:07:58,850 --> 00:08:00,907
One afternoon, on one of those drives,

200
00:08:00,927 --> 00:08:03,875
he saw a young woman on the side of the

201
00:08:03,895 --> 00:08:06,249
road, with the hood of her car open.

202
00:08:06,269 --> 00:08:08,029
He stopped and fixed the engine.

203
00:08:08,049 --> 00:08:10,403
Before they drove off, he asked if he

204
00:08:10,423 --> 00:08:11,610
could write to her.

205
00:08:12,825 --> 00:08:15,630
At first he told her he was a

206
00:08:15,650 --> 00:08:16,690
long-distance truck driver.

207
00:08:16,710 --> 00:08:19,868
In his first letter, he admitted he was a

208
00:08:19,888 --> 00:08:20,574
German prisoner.

209
00:08:20,594 --> 00:08:21,987
She wrote back anyway.

210
00:08:22,007 --> 00:08:24,812
She was a Christian, and she offered to

211
00:08:24,832 --> 00:08:26,245
send him a Bible.

212
00:08:27,625 --> 00:08:29,767
He arranged to be sent out picking cotton,

213
00:08:29,787 --> 00:08:32,470
on a farm where she could come and see him.

214
00:08:32,490 --> 00:08:34,362
And one day, in the cotton field,

215
00:08:34,382 --> 00:08:37,085
he told her that he had helped kill a man.

216
00:08:38,375 --> 00:08:40,953
She told him he had to confess.

217
00:08:40,973 --> 00:08:43,179
He fought it for several days.

218
00:08:43,199 --> 00:08:46,375
Then, near the end of 1945,

219
00:08:46,395 --> 00:08:48,902
he went to the American officer in charge

220
00:08:48,922 --> 00:08:51,429
of his labor camp and told him everything.

221
00:08:51,449 --> 00:08:53,640
The officer asked him if he understood

222
00:08:53,660 --> 00:08:55,535
what he was doing to himself.

223
00:08:55,555 --> 00:08:57,875
Meisel said he did.

224
00:08:59,550 --> 00:09:01,714
Meisel named the others.

225
00:09:01,734 --> 00:09:05,512
In January 1946, seven German prisoners

226
00:09:05,532 --> 00:09:08,082
went on trial before an American military

227
00:09:08,102 --> 00:09:10,285
court at Camp Swift, near Bastrop,

228
00:09:10,305 --> 00:09:12,875
Texas, for the murder of Hugo Krauss.

229
00:09:13,575 --> 00:09:15,660
The prosecutor told the court that two of

230
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:17,766
the accused had helped him break the case,

231
00:09:17,786 --> 00:09:20,170
and that without Meisel, the full story

232
00:09:20,190 --> 00:09:21,315
might never have come out.

233
00:09:22,550 --> 00:09:25,174
After more than two hours of deliberation,

234
00:09:25,194 --> 00:09:27,439
the court found all seven guilty.

235
00:09:27,459 --> 00:09:30,083
Each was sentenced to life in prison,

236
00:09:30,103 --> 00:09:31,216
at hard labor.

237
00:09:31,236 --> 00:09:33,859
They were sent to the federal penitentiary

238
00:09:33,879 --> 00:09:35,390
at Leavenworth, in Kansas.

239
00:09:36,775 --> 00:09:39,011
Then the sentences began to shrink.

240
00:09:39,031 --> 00:09:42,395
A review board threw out two of the convictions.

241
00:09:42,415 --> 00:09:45,999
Meisel's sentence was cut to fifteen

242
00:09:46,019 --> 00:09:47,962
years, the others to ten.

243
00:09:47,982 --> 00:09:51,535
In 1948, President Truman cut Meisel's to

244
00:09:51,555 --> 00:09:52,355
ten as well.

245
00:09:53,475 --> 00:09:57,475
In 1949, all five were paroled and sent

246
00:09:57,495 --> 00:09:58,685
home to Germany.

247
00:09:58,705 --> 00:10:01,912
They had spent about three years in prison.

248
00:10:01,932 --> 00:10:03,928
Fourteen German prisoners convicted in

249
00:10:03,948 --> 00:10:06,348
other camp murders had been hanged.

250
00:10:06,368 --> 00:10:09,595
The men who killed Hugo Krauss went home.

251
00:10:11,275 --> 00:10:13,593
Hugo Krauss was buried in the small

252
00:10:13,613 --> 00:10:15,915
cemetery at Camp Hearne.

253
00:10:15,935 --> 00:10:18,855
After the war, his body was moved to the

254
00:10:18,875 --> 00:10:20,815
national cemetery at Fort Sam Houston,

255
00:10:20,835 --> 00:10:23,428
in San Antonio, to a section with other

256
00:10:23,448 --> 00:10:24,755
German prisoners of war.

257
00:10:26,025 --> 00:10:29,645
Camp Hearne closed in 1946.

258
00:10:29,665 --> 00:10:31,216
The buildings were sold off,

259
00:10:31,236 --> 00:10:33,415
and the site sat empty for decades.

260
00:10:33,435 --> 00:10:35,615
Today there is a museum, a rebuilt

261
00:10:35,635 --> 00:10:37,814
barracks, and one of the little castles

262
00:10:37,834 --> 00:10:39,405
still standing in the grass.

263
00:10:40,750 --> 00:10:44,165
There is also a trail called the Scene of

264
00:10:44,185 --> 00:10:44,928
the Crime.

265
00:10:44,948 --> 00:10:47,218
Every December, near the anniversary of

266
00:10:47,238 --> 00:10:50,271
that night, the museum holds a walk there.

267
00:10:50,291 --> 00:10:52,560
Its program director said it plainly.

268
00:10:52,580 --> 00:10:54,870
He was done with Nazi Germany.

269
00:10:56,175 --> 00:10:58,433
If your family has a story about the

270
00:10:58,453 --> 00:11:00,141
prisoner of war camps in Texas,

271
00:11:00,161 --> 00:11:02,575
or about Camp Hearne, tell us in the comments.

272
00:11:04,350 --> 00:11:07,073
In San Antonio, his stone stands in a row

273
00:11:07,093 --> 00:11:09,816
of German names, far from New York and far

274
00:11:09,836 --> 00:11:10,426
from Germany.

